📌 Exercise 3: User-Defined Functions (UDFs) & Pandas UDFs
👉 Task:
Create a UDF to categorize salaries:
Salary < 6000 → "Low"
Salary ≥ 6000 → "High"


Convert this function to a Pandas UDF for better performance.


✍ Write your code below:
python
CopyEdit
from pyspark.sql.functions import udf
from pyspark.sql.types import StringType
import pandas as pd
from pyspark.sql.functions import pandas_udf

# Define UDF
def salary_category(salary):
    return "High" if salary >= 6000 else "Low"

# Register UDF
category_udf = udf(salary_category, StringType())

# Apply UDF
df = df.withColumn("category", category_udf(df.salary))
df.show()

# Convert to Pandas UDF
@pandas_udf(StringType())
def salary_category_pandas(salary_series: pd.Series) -> pd.Series:
    return salary_series.apply(lambda x: "High" if x >= 6000 else "Low")

# Apply Pandas UDF
df = df.withColumn("category_pandas", salary_category_pandas(df.salary))
df.show()

✅ Why Pandas UDF?
Faster execution
Vectorized operations (better performance than row-wise UDFs)



